if you love flying as much as I do your  priority will always be getting that  window seat some of my favorite  experiences from traveling have been  experienced through the window of a  plane from looking down on the Burj  Khalifa to returning to the green fields  of Ireland flying has always been a  special experience for me so when I got  a chance to fly in the Boeing 787  dreamliner with its huge windows I was  extremely excited I've been waiting for  years to get the opportunity to fly in  this groundbreaking plane - some of you  it may just look like another everyday  passenger plane but the 787 is a  revolutionary plane the Dreamliner was  the first commercial passenger plane to  fly with the majority of its structure  being built with CompStat materials  mostly carbon reinforced plastics this  reduced the weight of the aircraft  dramatically helping it be around 20%  more fuel efficient than a similar sized  aircraft that 20% increase in fuel  efficiency isn't just saving you and the  airline money but it has allowed new  flight routes that were previously  financially unviable to open you see the  787 is not a huge plane it can carry  less than half the passengers than the  double-decker Airbus a380 and fills the  size gap between the long-range Boeing  777 and the short to mid-range 737  boeing specifically built this plane to  serve more niche routes that larger  planes could not serve as the amount of  people flying between the locations was  too low to sell the number of tickets  needed to make it profitable airlines  call these long thin routes want to fly  from Dublin to Los Angeles directly the  787 made the possible no more stopping  in London or New York visibility may  have even saved Polish Airlines from  bankruptcy as a Dreamliner finally  allowed them to profitably fly directly  from their warsaw hub to New York  Chicago and Toronto this is great for  Airlines and great for any of us who  live in relatively small cities that  usually have to travel to the nearest  large airport to fly to our final  destination but my favorite thing about  the Boeing 787 are those huge windows  the 787's windows are this much larger  than an average airplane window if  you're at anything like me you will  always end up with your face crammed  next to the window trying to get the  best view possible  or the 787 you can see so much while  sitting back and relaxing it really  gives you this wonderful panoramic view  feeling it also has these electronic  dimmers which do a with the need for  those crappy plastic shutters that  people insist on opening while others  are trying to sleep that isn't possible  with the 787 as the master dimming  switch is controlled by the flight  attendants so you may be wondering why  are these windows so much larger than  other planes this is another advantage  of compsat materials you may remember  from my first video early passenger  planes like the de Havilland comet had a  series of unfortunate midair explosions  after intensive research the  investigators discovered that the square  windows were blocking the flow of stress  in the structure making it pile up in  the corners of the window round windows  allowed the stress to flow much more  smoothly around the windows and thus our  windows have been rounded ever since but  the shape is not the only thing that can  elevate stress concentrations the size  of the window has a huge effect on a to  a larger window means more stress has to  detour around the window and thus the  stress concentration is elevated which  can result in cracks and failures so  these large windows do result in higher  levels of stress concentration around  the 787 windows but I failed to mention  one factor in that short little  two-minute video a year ago fatigue over  the course of the planes life it is  repeatedly pressurized and unpressurized  as a rises and falls through the  atmosphere this means the material  experiences varying stresses continually  over its life that can damage it this  was the second piece of the puzzle for  the de Havilland comet mystery and its  engineers proved it by building this  huge water tank where pressurization  tests could be done quickly the test  fuselage had undergone 1230 normal  flights and 1830 tank flights before it  failed at the corner of the square  windows this is why parts need to be  replaced after years of use apparently  handled a single application of a load  without any problem but after many  cycles of loading the material will  begin to weaken as tiny cracks form in  the metallic structure metals like  aluminium which is traditionally used  for aircraft fuselage have a natural  tendency to form cracks over time this  is because small defects in the crystal  structure called dislocations which are  essentially holes in the atomic  structure create weakness that makes it  easier to deform these dislocations can  travel through the metal structure and  how  tendency to coalesce and so after  hundreds of cycles what was once a small  defect can grow into a large defect in  the form of a crack and these cracks  cause failure of the parrot fatigue  failure in comps and materials is  incredibly complex and we still have a  lot to learn about the subject I have no  problem admitting right now I don't  understand it enough to teach any of you  of confidence on the details I have a  thesis specializing in the subject but I  can say this with confidence fatigue  failure does not happen as quickly in  carbon-fiber-reinforced fuselages and  there are many tests to prove that the  787 fuselage is manufactured by wrapping  layer after layer of resin so carbon  fiber around the fuselage many times  this creates a structure that is very  resistant to fatigue as the  high-strength fibers are wrapped  continuously around the fuselage fatigue  failure will eventually occur in the  plastic resin holding the fibers  together but it takes a great deal  longer than an aluminium this allows the  carbon reinforced plastic frame of the  787 to tolerate higher stresses in the  fuselage and lasts longer doing it there  is still a lot to be learned about the  mechanics of compsat materials compsat  materials are a difficult material to  work with at the best of times and we  will be learning more about that in the  next video where we will be learning  more about the history of the material  and I will be visiting a local composit  Factory to show you more about the  manufacturing process thanks for  watching this episode is brought to you  by Squarespace who have helped me make  my next move with my website one of my  big goals for this year is to finally  create the beautiful website that I  envisioned when I first started this  project I am in the process of  relaunching the real engineering website  and will be continually improving it  over the next year and will be able to  share my progress at the end of videos  thanks to Squarespace  Squarespace allows you to create a  beautiful website or online store with  award-winning templates they have 24/7  customer service and their intuitive  design platform makes it easy to create  the website you dreamed of you can make  your next move with Squarespace and  build a beautiful website by using the  code real engineering for 10% off your  next purchase  thank you Squarespace for helping real  engineering exist both here on YouTube  and on our beautiful new website and  thank you all to my viewers and patreon  supporters to one of my patreon  supporters actually reached out to help  me build this site being able to  interact with you all there has been  amazing  and the feedback you give me on early  drafts of my videos is priceless thank  you last week I introduced a new reward  in the form of the real engineering  gear-tooth logo t-shirt which you can  get on patreon or over a dftba.com if  you'd like to see more from me the links  from my patreon Facebook Twitter and  Instagram accounts are in the  description
 

